export const seedPages: Record<string, string> = {
  "ai-agents.md": "# Running AI agents in Flisk\n\nFlisk is the local runtime for agent **systems**: your agent process, its local state store, its MCP tool servers, databases, queues, and mock providers, running behind one gateway with one command: `flisk up`.\n\nInstead of running agents as detached scripts or hand-rolling complex Compose files, Flisk gives agents first-class local infrastructure:\n- **Durable checkpoints**: Session state survives `flisk down`, container restarts, and hot reload via persistent named volumes.\n- **Graceful SIGTERM drains**: In-flight LLM turns checkpoint before shutdown or live-update reloads.\n- **Session routing**: Traefik routes requests by session (`x-session-id`), keeping concurrent agent conversations isolated.\n- **Liveness and application readiness**: `/health` confirms process liveness. `/ready` verifies the checkpoint store for callers that need to wait before sending turns. Flisk's generated health check uses `healthCheckPath`, which is `/health` in this example.\n\nThe product website already publishes a site-level [llms.txt](https://flisk.sh/llms.txt). The files in this folder give agents browsing the source repository a CLI-focused contract.\n\n---\n\n## Three ways to run or use agents with Flisk\n\n### 1. The first-class `agent` resource type (Recommended)\n\nScaffold a dedicated agent service with durability and session management:\n\n```bash\nflisk resource bot --type agent --stack shop --yes\nflisk up shop\n```\n\nThis creates a Bun HTTP service in `services/shop/bot` with `service.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://flisk.sh/schemas/service-schema.json\",\n  \"name\": \"bot\",\n  \"appType\": \"agent\",\n  \"stack\": \"shop\",\n  \"port\": 4001,\n  \"healthCheckPath\": \"/health\",\n  \"agent\": {\n    \"runtime\": \"custom\",\n    \"durability\": \"files\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### What you get\n- **Custom runtime escape hatch**: `src/index.ts` is vendor-neutral (plain Node/Bun built-ins). Call Anthropic, OpenAI, Gemini, LangGraph, or Mastra inside `runTurn(checkpoint, input)`.\n- **Files durability volume**: Checkpoints are stored in `/var/lib/flisk-agent/<sessionId>.json` on a named Docker volume (`<project>-<stack>-autogenerated_<res>-agent-state`). The volume survives `flisk down`. Remove it explicitly with `docker volume rm <project>-<stack>-autogenerated_<res>-agent-state`.\n- **Session-aware turn execution**: Send a POST to `http://api.shop.localhost/api/bot/turn` with `x-session-id: session-123`. The agent loads the session checkpoint, appends the user prompt, invokes `runTurn()`, atomically commits the updated checkpoint, and returns the reply.\n- **Session list**: `GET http://api.shop.localhost/api/bot/sessions` (no `x-session-id`) returns `{ \"sessions\": [{ \"sessionId\", \"lastActivity\", \"status\", \"checkpointBytes\" }], \"total\": n }`, newest first, at most 500 rows (`total` counts all). `status` is `active` while this instance runs a turn for the session, otherwise `idle`. It reads only checkpoint metadata. `flisk agent sessions <resource>` prints it as a table (`--watch` refreshes, `--json` returns it under `data.sessions`); `--session-id <id>` reads one checkpoint instead. An agent scaffolded before this endpoint answers 404, and the CLI reports `SESSION_LIST_UNSUPPORTED`: copy the `/sessions` handler from a new scaffold into `src/index.ts`.\n- **Application readiness check**: `GET /ready` verifies that the state directory is accessible and writable before callers send turns. It is not the endpoint used by Flisk's generated health check in this example.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Agent as an `mcp` resource\n\nExpose local tools, databases, and microservices to coding agents (Claude Code, Codex, Cursor) via the Model Context Protocol:\n\n```bash\nflisk resource tools-mcp --type mcp --stack shop --yes\nflisk up shop\n```\n\n- Generates an MCP server built with `@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` on the Streamable HTTP transport at `/mcp`.\n- Runs alongside your application services under Traefik routing at `http://api.shop.localhost/api/tools-mcp/mcp`.\n- Lets external agents safely query your local database or trigger application endpoints through standardized MCP tools.\n- See [docs/mcp.md](../mcp.md) for full details on tool registration and schemas.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Flisk as the control plane via `flisk mcp`\n\nLet your AI coding assistant orchestrate your local stack directly through MCP over stdio:\n\nConfigure `flisk mcp` in your agent's MCP settings (e.g., `~/.claude/claude_desktop_config.json` or `.cursor/mcp.json`):\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"flisk\": {\n      \"command\": \"flisk\",\n      \"args\": [\"mcp\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nNow Claude Code, Cursor, or Codex can:\n- Run `doctor` to diagnose container runtime or port conflicts.\n- Call `up` / `down` to launch or teardown services safely.\n- Read structured `status` to verify whether APIs and databases are healthy.\n- Inspect `logs` with bounded windows to debug failures without dumping mega-token stack traces into context.\n- Create the project and its services with `project_init`, `resource_create` and `stack_assign`, then check the result with `config_verify`.\n\nThe setup guide lists every tool: [docs/agent-tooling/flisk-mcp-setup.md](../agent-tooling/flisk-mcp-setup.md#the-tools).\n\n---\n\n## Agent recipes with existing service types\n\nIf you have existing worker pipelines or custom Python/Go agents, you can also host them using standard Flisk primitives:\n\n### Recipe: Python / LangGraph agent as a `worker` with Postgres\n\nFor background agents processing task queues or event loops:\n\n```bash\nflisk resource research-agent --type worker --stack shop --yes\n```\n\nIn `service.json`, add dependency links to your database or message queue:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"research-agent\",\n  \"appType\": \"worker\",\n  \"stack\": \"shop\",\n  \"dependsOn\": [\"postgres\", \"nats\"],\n  \"env\": {\n    \"DATABASE_URL\": \"@ref(postgres.DATABASE_URL)\",\n    \"NATS_URL\": \"@ref(nats.NATS_URL)\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThe agent boots only after Postgres and NATS pass their readiness gates, receives clean database credentials, and reconnects smoothly during hot reload.\n\n---\n\n## How Flisk routes agent traffic\n\nTraefik serves as the unified local gateway for your entire agent topology:\n\n```text\n                          +--------------------------+\n                          |     Traefik Gateway      |\n                          |   (*.localhost:80/8080)  |\n                          +----------+---------------+\n                                     |\n              /api/bot/*             |             /api/tools-mcp/mcp\n         (x-session-id sticky)       |                    |\n                                     v                    v\n                          +----------------+     +----------------+\n                          |  agent (bot)   |     |  mcp resource  |\n                          | Bun / Lang-    |     |  Tools server  |\n                          | Graph / Mastra |     +--------+-------+\n                          +--------+-------+              |\n                                   |                      |\n                                   v                      v\n                          +--------------------------------+\n                          |  Postgres / NATS / Backend     |\n                          +--------------------------------+\n```\n\n- **Clean hostnames**: Reach your agents and APIs through `api.<project>.localhost`.\n- **Zero port conflicts**: Even with dozens of tools, agents, and backends running, Traefik multiplexes everything through a single host port.\n\nThe product website already publishes a site-level [llms.txt](https://flisk.sh/llms.txt).\n",
  "byo.md": "# Bring-Your-Own Resources\n\nWrap an existing service with Flisk orchestration without generating application code.\n\n## Command\n\n```bash\nflisk resource <name> --type bring-your-own --stack <stack>\n```\n\nAliases: `byo`, `bring-your-own`\n\n## service.json Example\n\n```json\n{\n  \"appName\": \"legacy\",\n  \"appType\": \"bring-your-own\",\n  \"stack\": \"shop\",\n  \"port\": 4500,\n  \"healthCheckPath\": \"/health\",\n  \"dockerfile\": \"./Dockerfile\"\n}\n```\n\n## Notes\n\n- Flisk does not generate application code for this type\n- Provide your own Dockerfile or use `--image <name>` to use an existing image\n- Default port range: 4000-5999 (next free port)\n- If Dockerfile exists, it will not be overwritten\n- BYO resources receive the standard Traefik route by default. Use `--no-proxy` to disable it;\n  this writes `exposeViaProxy: false` to the service manifest\n- Use `--yes` in scripts to skip the create confirmation\n- `--port` accepts an unused integer from 4000 through 5999\n- By default, Flisk writes an Nginx Dockerfile and health endpoint that listen on the assigned\n  service port\n- Set `\"buildContext\"` in `service.json` (a path relative to the resource directory) to build from a source tree elsewhere in the repo, as `flisk-import` does for an existing Dockerfile. `dockerfile` is then relative to that context. Without it the context is the resource directory\n- Pass `--dockerfile <path>` to select a custom Dockerfile, or `--image <image>` to use an\n  existing image without creating a Dockerfile\n\n## One-shot jobs (migrations, seeders)\n\nA bring-your-own container is restarted with `unless-stopped` by default, so a job that exits `0` is started again forever.\nFor a one-shot job, set the restart policy to `no`:\n\n```bash\nflisk resource migrate --type bring-your-own --stack shop --dockerfile ./Dockerfile --restart no --yes\n```\n\n`--restart` takes `no`, `on-failure`, `unless-stopped` (the default) or `always`, and is written to `service.json` as\n`\"restart\": \"no\"`. It is rejected for any other resource type. Checked with a real `flisk up` (PR #275): a dbmate job\n(`ghcr.io/amacneil/dbmate:2`, `dbmate up`) with `restart: \"no\"` applied its migration once and stayed `exited (0)` with zero restarts. A runnable version lives in [`examples/byo-jobs/`](../examples/byo-jobs/README.md); check it with `tests/e2e/resource-types/bring-your-own/verify-byo-job.sh dbmate`.\n\n## Bringing a framework app (Node, Go, Rust, ...)\n\nAny app that fits the contract below can run as a bring-your-own resource. Fastify is the worked example because it needs nothing beyond the framework.\n\n### What Flisk needs from the container\n\n1. **Listen on the port in `service.json`.** The generated Compose service sets `PORT` to that value, and also sets `DATABASE_URL`, so read the port from the environment instead of hard-coding it.\n2. **Bind to `0.0.0.0`.** Traefik reaches the container over the Docker network, so a server that only binds `127.0.0.1` is unreachable.\n3. **Answer HTTP 200 on `healthCheckPath`, if the service speaks HTTP.** Set it with `--health-path`. Traefik uses this check. Flisk does not add a Compose `curl` health check for bring-your-own services, so the image does not need `curl`. A service that is not HTTP (a database, a mail relay, an SSH host) can leave `healthCheckPath` out of `service.json`: Flisk then sends no HTTP probe to it and no Traefik health check, and the container counts as healthy once Docker reports it running.\n4. **Provide the image.** Either a `Dockerfile` or `--image`. The Docker build context is the resource directory, so `COPY` paths are relative to it.\n\n### Worked example: Fastify\n\n```text\nservices/shop/orders-api/\n  Dockerfile\n  package.json\n  server.js\n```\n\n```js\n// server.js\nimport Fastify from 'fastify';\nconst app = Fastify({ logger: true });\napp.get('/health', async () => ({ status: 'ok' }));\napp.get('/orders', async () => [{ id: 1, item: 'tea' }]);\nawait app.listen({ port: Number(process.env.PORT ?? 3000), host: '0.0.0.0' });\n```\n\n```dockerfile\n# Dockerfile\nFROM node:22-alpine\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package.json ./\nRUN npm install --omit=dev\nCOPY server.js ./\nCMD [\"node\", \"server.js\"]\n```\n\n```bash\nflisk resource orders-api --type bring-your-own --stack shop \\\n  --dockerfile ./Dockerfile --health-path /health \\\n  --path services/shop/orders-api --yes\nflisk up shop\n```\n\nThe existing `Dockerfile` is kept, and the resource gets a `service.json` like the one at the top of this page (`appType: bring-your-own`, a port from 4000-5999, `healthCheckPath`, `dockerfile`).\n\n### Limits\n\n- **No live reload.** Flisk does not sync source into a bring-your-own container, and only the manifest is watched. After changing source, rebuild the image from the Tilt UI or restart `flisk up`. For Go, the native provider has an opt-in loop (`dev.liveReload`, see [backend language providers](backend-language-providers.md#opt-in-live-reload)); a Go app you bring as a container, including the Gin example, does not get it.\n- **Ports.** 4000-5999 for this type, shared with backends.\n- **No generated code.** Flisk will not add a health route, a `PORT` read or a Dockerfile for you.\n\n### Examples and recipes\n\n[`examples/byo/`](../examples/byo/README.md) has one folder per framework, each with a Dockerfile, its own `README.md`\nand a check command:\n\n```bash\ntests/e2e/resource-types/bring-your-own/verify-byo-example.sh fastify          # health path /health\nscripts/sync-byo-templates.sh vue && tests/e2e/resource-types/bring-your-own/verify-byo-example.sh create-vue /   # synced template; Vite apps answer on /\ntests/e2e/resource-types/bring-your-own/verify-byo-flisk.sh full-stack-fastapi-template /api/v1/utils/health-check/   # needs a database: real flisk up\nVERIFY_WAIT_SECONDS=120 tests/e2e/resource-types/bring-your-own/verify-byo-example.sh create-mastra /health   # slow starters\n```\n\nThe check builds the image, runs it with `PORT=4000` and expects HTTP 200. Folders named `create-*`, `ember`, `rsbuild`\nand similar run the real scaffolder at build time, so they stay in step with it.\nThe `create-*` folders listed in `examples/byo/templates.json` (`create-vue`, `create-next-app`, `create-adonisjs`,\n`create-analog`, `create-bati`) come from their own template repositories at the tag pinned in\n[`examples/byo/templates.json`](../examples/byo/templates.json); run `scripts/sync-byo-templates.sh` to copy them in first.\n\nRecipes for using Flisk next to other tools live in [`recipes/`](recipes/): [moon](recipes/moon.md), [Rsbuild](recipes/rsbuild.md).\n\n### Running more than one project\n\n- **Resource names must be unique across Flisk projects that share one Docker daemon.** Traefik watches every container,\n  so two projects that both define `orders-api` clash and routes are dropped.\n- **Docker can run out of address pools.** Each project creates several networks. When Docker answers\n  `all predefined address pools have been fully subnetted`, the `init-networks` resource fails with Docker's message\n  (`Failed to create Docker network <name>: ...`) and the services that need the network do not start. Free unused networks with `flisk down --prune-networks` in projects you no longer use (or `docker network prune` for every unused network).\n- Through `flisk up`, a service is routed at `http://api.<project>.localhost/api/<name without -api>/...` with the prefix\n  stripped, so a frontend served under that path needs its `base` set.\n\n### What only `flisk up` shows\n\nRunning an image with `docker run` is not enough; these were found by running the example through a real `flisk up` and Traefik:\n\n- **Flisk sets a Postgres `DATABASE_URL` in every bring-your-own container,** even when the app has no database. A framework that\n  prefers it over its own config can fail: the [Rails example](../examples/byo/rails/README.md) answered 500 (`pg is not part of the\n  bundle`) until the server started with `DATABASE_URL` unset.\n- **The `Host` header is `api.<project>.localhost`.** Rails' development host check blocked it (`Blocked hosts: ...`) until the example\n  allowed any host. Most other examples accepted it with no change (Angular, Flask, Gin, Symfony, Quarkus, Micronaut, Echo, chi,\n  Sinatra, Docusaurus, SolidStart, Phoenix). That list is the examples checked through `flisk up`; the others have not been.\n- **A cold Docker cache is slow.** `flisk up` builds Flisk's shared base images the first time, so give a first check several minutes.\n\nOne more trap shows up with a plain `docker run` as well, if you read the log: **a generator can write a loopback-only bind inside\n`docker build`.** The [Phoenix example](../examples/byo/phoenix/README.md) failed its first check because the generator wrote\n`127.0.0.1` in the image build but `0.0.0.0` when run inside a started container, so its Dockerfile rewrites the line and fails the build\nif the result is not there.\n\n### What has and has not been checked\n\nChecked: each folder under `examples/byo/` builds and answers `200` on its health path when run with `PORT=4000`. Many also ran\nthrough a full `flisk up` and Traefik; each folder's own `README.md` says which check it passed and what it did not cover, so look there\nrather than at a list here. A persistent moon task ran `flisk up` (see the moon recipe).\n\nNot checked: a production image for any example. Most examples run a dev server, and none of them was load-tested.\n",
  "cheat-sheet.md": "# ⚡ Flisk mega cheat sheet\n\n> Every `flisk` command, alias and flag on one page. Taken from `flisk <command> --help` for **v1.3.125**. Your version may differ, so run `flisk <command> --help` to check.\n\n**Jump to:** [🚀 Start](#-first-five-minutes) · [🔥 Top 10](#-top-10) · [🗺️ Map](#️-how-it-fits-together) · [🌍 Project](#-project) · [📦 Resources](#-resources) · [🗂️ Stacks](#️-stacks) · [▶️ Lifecycle](#️-lifecycle) · [⚙️ Config](#️-configuration) · [🩺 Diagnostics](#-diagnostics-and-tooling) · [🤖 JSON](#-json-output-and-exit-codes) · [🌱 Env](#-environment-variables) · [📁 Files](#-files-flisk-writes) · [🍳 Recipes](#-recipes)\n\nWant the short version? See the [10-row cheat sheet](../cli/README.md#cheat-sheet).\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Needs Docker (Engine 25+, Compose 2.20.2+), [Tilt](https://docs.tilt.dev/install.html), and Bun 1.2+ for the default generated services. On Windows, run the CLI in WSL2 ([setup](wsl2.md)).\n\n## 🚀 First five minutes\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://flisk.sh/install.sh | sh   # install\nflisk doctor                                                  # Docker, Tilt and Bun OK?\nflisk project --yes                                           # create the project\nflisk resource api --type backend --stack api --yes           # a Bun + Hono API\nflisk resource web --type frontend --stack api --yes          # a React app\nflisk up api                                                  # start the stack\nflisk networks                                                # where is everything?\nflisk down                                                    # stop it all\n```\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Run `flisk` with no arguments for a quick readiness check. It exits 1 if your machine is not ready.\n\n## 🔥 Top 10\n\n| You want to… | Run |\n| --- | --- |\n| Start everything | `flisk up` |\n| Start one service and what it needs | `flisk up --only orders-api` |\n| See what is running | `flisk status --tilt` |\n| Get the URLs | `flisk networks --raw` |\n| Read recent logs | `flisk logs -s orders-api --since 5m` |\n| Add a service | `flisk resource <name> --type backend --yes` |\n| Group services | `flisk stack api --resources a b --yes` |\n| Fix \"my machine is weird\" | `flisk doctor` |\n| Fix \"generated files drifted\" | `flisk config regenerate` |\n| Stop everything | `flisk down` |\n\n## 🗺️ How it fits together\n\nA **project** holds **resources** (services). **Stacks** group resources so you start them together.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  A[flisk project] --> B[flisk resource]\n  I[flisk import] --> B\n  B --> C[flisk stack]\n  C --> D[flisk up]\n  D --> E[flisk status / logs / networks]\n  E --> F[flisk down]\n  D -. drift? .-> G[flisk config regenerate]\n  G --> D\n```\n\n## ⬆️ Install and update\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://flisk.sh/install.sh | sh\nflisk upgrade                 # alias: flisk update\nflisk upgrade --dry-run       # show what would change\nflisk upgrade --force --yes   # reinstall even if already on latest, no prompt\nflisk version                 # aliases: flisk v, flisk -v, flisk --version\n```\n\n## 🌐 Global options\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `-v`, `--version` | Print the version |\n| `--verbose` | Verbose output |\n| `-h`, `--help` | Help. `flisk help <command>` works too |\n| `flisk --doctor` | Same as `flisk doctor` |\n\n## 📋 Commands at a glance\n\n| Command | Aliases | What it does |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `flisk project [template]` | `init` | Create or check the project's master configs, or clone a starter |\n| `flisk projects` | `info` | Show project info and config status |\n| `flisk import [dir]` | | Import the services in a directory's root `docker-compose.yml` |\n| `flisk eject` | | Write `EJECTED.md` describing the generated Tilt files |\n| `flisk resource [name]` | | Create a resource, or register an existing one |\n| `flisk resources` | | List resources |\n| `flisk stack [stack-name]` | | Assign resources to a stack |\n| `flisk stacks` | `ls` | List stacks |\n| `flisk up [stack-name]` | `deploy` | Start services |\n| `flisk down` | | Stop services |\n| `flisk status` | | Show stack and service state |\n| `flisk logs` | | Print recent logs from the running stack |\n| `flisk networks` | `urls`, `traefik` | Show Traefik-routed URLs |\n| `flisk config <subcommand>` | | Regenerate, verify, migrate or edit project config |\n| `flisk doctor` | | Check environment readiness |\n| `flisk ui` | `interactive` | Interactive terminal UI |\n| `flisk mcp` | | MCP server over stdio for coding agents |\n| `flisk runtime` | | Inspect packaged engine and template assets |\n| `flisk completion [shell]` | | Shell completion scripts |\n| `flisk upgrade` | `update` | Self-update |\n| `flisk version` | `v` | Print the version |\n| `flisk maintainers check [file]` | | Check `MAINTAINERS.md` |\n\n## 🌍 Project\n\n### `flisk project [template]` (alias: `flisk init`)\n\n```bash\nflisk project                          # interactive\nflisk project --yes                    # defaults, no prompts\nflisk project --check                  # do master configs exist and match?\nflisk project --config-file cfg.json   # load project config from JSON\nflisk project saas                     # clone a starter example\nflisk project saas --path my-app       # clone into my-app/\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `[template]` | Clone a starter instead of a blank project: `restaurant`, `saas`, `erp`, `user-management`, `ecommerce`, `example` |\n| `--path <dir>` | Directory to clone the template into (default: the template's repo name) |\n| `--check` | Check that master configs exist and are in sync |\n| `--force` | Overwrite existing configuration ⚠️ destructive |\n| `--yes` | Non-interactive, use defaults |\n| `--config-file <path>` | Load project config from an existing JSON file |\n| `--no-agent-skills` | Do not write the Flisk agent skills (`.claude/skills`, `.agents/skills`) and `AGENTS.md` |\n\nCreates `TILT_RESOURCE_DEFAULTS.star` and `TILT_TECH_STACK.star`, among others. A new project also gets the Flisk agent skills in `.claude/skills` and `.agents/skills` and a short `AGENTS.md`, so a coding agent opened there knows how to build and run the app; files that already exist are left as they are. See [configuration](configuration.md) and [the tech stack guide](tech-stack-lock.md).\n\nProject setup does not need a running Docker daemon or an installed Tilt: `flisk project` writes the project configuration and clones the template, then prints what to start before `flisk up`. `flisk up` still refuses to start without the local runtime.\n\n### `flisk projects` (alias `info`)\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--check` | Validate configuration; exit 0 or 1. Use in CI |\n| `--json` | Versioned JSON report |\n\n### `flisk import [dir]`\n\nRead the root `docker-compose.yml` of a directory (default `.`) and write one bring-your-own `service.json` per Compose service. Other Compose file names, override files, Dockerfiles and `package.json` are not read, and the plan names what was not translated.\n\n```bash\nflisk import --dry-run                 # print the plan, write nothing\nflisk import ./legacy --yes\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--dry-run` | Print the plan and write nothing |\n| `--yes` | Write without prompting |\n| `--force` | Overwrite an existing `service.json` |\n\nSee [gradual adoption](gradual-adoption.md) and [adopt Flisk](adopt-flisk.md).\n\n### `flisk eject`\n\nWrites `EJECTED.md` describing the generated Tilt files. It copies nothing; the files stay in `.flisk/.flisk-out/`.\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--dry-run` | List the generated files and what would be written |\n| `--yes` | Skip the confirmation prompt |\n\nSee [leaving Flisk](leaving-flisk.md).\n\n## 📦 Resources\n\n### `flisk resource [name]`\n\n```bash\nflisk resource api --type backend --yes                         # Bun + Hono\nflisk resource api --type backend                                # asks for the framework (Hono first)\nflisk resource api --type backend --framework fastify --yes\nflisk resource api --type backend --language python --yes\nflisk resource api --type backend --feature prisma --yes\nflisk resource web --type frontend --framework vue --yes\nflisk resource --frameworks                                     # list frontend frameworks\nflisk resource --type frontend --frameworks                     # list frontend frameworks\nflisk resource --type backend --frameworks                      # list backend frameworks and their language\nflisk resource --type backend --language python --frameworks     # list backend frameworks, marking the incompatible ones\nflisk resource jobs --type worker --yes\nflisk resource tools --type mcp --yes\nflisk resource legacy --type bring-your-own --dockerfile Dockerfile --health-path /healthz\nflisk resource cache --type bring-your-own --image redis:7 --no-proxy\nflisk resource old-api --path services/old-api --register-existing\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `-t`, `--type <type>` | `backend` (default), `frontend`, `worker`, `mcp`, `bring-your-own`, `sdk` |\n| `--framework <id>` | Frontend: `react` (default), `vue`, `svelte`, `preact`, `lit`, `solid`, `qwik`, `vanilla`, `tanstack-router`, `next` (Next.js App Router, a Node server). Backend: `hono` (default), `express`, `elysia`, `fastify`, `nestjs`, `koa`, `h3` |\n| `--frameworks` | List registered frameworks for the resource type and exit (`--type frontend`, `--type backend`) |\n| `--language <id>` | Backend language: `bun` (default), `python`, `go`, `rust`, `node` |\n| `-s`, `--stack <stack>` | Stack to assign the resource to |\n| `-p`, `--path <path>` | Custom resource directory. `--resource-path` is an older alias |\n| `--register-existing` | Register an existing resource without creating templates |\n| `-y`, `--yes` | No prompts, use defaults |\n| `--port <port>` | Port (default: next free in 4000-5999) |\n| `--feature <feature...>` | Enable a resource feature, for example `prisma` |\n| `--ddd` | Domain-driven design folders and path aliases. Premium, needs `FLISK_LICENSE_KEY` |\n\nBring-your-own only:\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--dockerfile <path>` | Dockerfile path, relative to the resource directory |\n| `--image <name>` | Use a Docker image instead of building a Dockerfile |\n| `--health-path <path>` | HTTP health check path (default `/health`) |\n| `--no-proxy` | No Traefik route |\n| `--restart <policy>` | Compose restart policy: `no`, `on-failure`, `unless-stopped`, `always`. Use `no` for one-shot jobs |\n\nSee [backend languages](backend-language-providers.md), [frontend frameworks](frontend-framework-providers.md), [bring-your-own](byo.md), [MCP resources](mcp.md) and [data](data.md).\n\n### `flisk resources`\n\n```bash\nflisk resources --ports\nflisk resources --stack api --type backend\nflisk resources --no-stack          # resources without a stack\nflisk resources --json\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `-s`, `--stack <stack>` | Filter by stack |\n| `-t`, `--type <type>` | Filter by type |\n| `--no-stack` | Only resources without a stack |\n| `--ports` | Show port assignments |\n| `-v`, `--verbose` | More detail per resource |\n| `--json` | Versioned JSON report |\n\n## 🗂️ Stacks\n\n### `flisk stack [stack-name]`\n\n```bash\nflisk stack api                                         # pick resources interactively\nflisk stack api --resources orders-api users-api --yes  # scriptable\nflisk stack api --resources orders-api,users-api --yes\nflisk stack --list                                      # resources without a stack\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--resources <names...>` | Resources to add (comma- or space-separated) |\n| `--list` | List resources without a stack |\n| `--yes` | Skip the confirmation prompt |\n\n### `flisk stacks` (alias `ls`)\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--services` | Include each stack's resources |\n| `-v`, `--verbose` | More detail per stack |\n| `--json` | Versioned JSON report |\n\n## ▶️ Lifecycle\n\n### `flisk up [stack-name]` (alias `deploy`)\n\n```bash\nflisk up                         # everything\nflisk up api                     # one stack\nflisk up api --only orders-api   # one service plus its dependsOn and shared infra\nflisk up --dry-run\nflisk up --force                 # kill an existing Tilt process first\nflisk up --json &                # one JSON object when ready; Tilt keeps running\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--only <services...>` | Start only these services, plus their `dependsOn` services and shared infrastructure. With a stack, names must belong to it |\n| `--dry-run` | Show what would start |\n| `-f`, `--force` | Kill an existing Tilt process before starting |\n| `--ignore-drift` | Start even if generated files differ from `service.json` |\n| `--ignore-version` | Start even if this `flisk` is older than `minFliskVersion` in `.flisk/project.json` |\n| `--json` | Print one JSON object when the stack is ready or the command fails. Implies `--quiet` |\n| `-q`, `--quiet` | Less output |\n| `-v`, `--verbose` | More output |\n\n> [!TIP]\n> `flisk up` prints the host ports and URLs it chose. A resource named `orders-api` is served at `/api/orders`, because a trailing `-api` is dropped.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> `flisk up --json` returns once the stack is ready, and Tilt keeps running. Run it in the background (`&`).\n\n### `flisk down`\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `-f`, `--force` | Accepted for script compatibility; has no effect |\n| `--prune-networks` | Also remove this project's Docker networks that no container uses |\n| `--dry-run` | Show what would stop |\n| `--json` | One JSON object on stdout |\n| `-v`, `--verbose` | More output |\n\n### `flisk status`\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--stacks` | Stack information (the default) |\n| `--resources` | All discovered resources |\n| `--tilt` | Tilt resource status |\n| `--unhealthy` | Only failing or pending services (says so when all are healthy) |\n| `-v`, `--verbose` | More detail |\n| `--json` | Versioned JSON report |\n\n### `flisk logs`\n\nA bounded snapshot of recent logs. It does not follow new output; for live logs, use the Tilt UI.\n\n```bash\nflisk logs\nflisk logs -s orders-api users-api --tail 50\nflisk logs --since 5m --json\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `-s`, `--service <names...>` | Only these services (Tilt resource names) |\n| `--unhealthy` | Only services that are failing or still starting |\n| `--tail <n>` | Most recent lines (default 200) |\n| `--since <duration>` | Only logs newer than this, for example `30s`, `5m`, `1h` |\n| `--port <n>` | Tilt UI port (default `TILT_PORT` or 10350) |\n| `--json` | One JSON object |\n\n### `flisk networks` (aliases `urls`, `traefik`)\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `-s`, `--stack <stack>` | Filter by stack |\n| `--raw` | URLs only, one per line |\n| `--json` | Versioned JSON report |\n| `--json-legacy` | Old JSON array (deprecated) |\n\n## ⚙️ Configuration\n\n```bash\nflisk config regenerate --dry-run      # preview changes to the 4 master files\nflisk config regenerate                # rewrite them from .flisk/project.json\nflisk config verify --json             # do generated files match project.json?\nflisk config explain <file>            # is this file generated, and does editing it block flisk up?\nflisk config migrate                   # move service.json files to the current schema\nflisk config edit                      # open .flisk/project.json in $EDITOR\nflisk config enable-infra monitoring\nflisk config disable-infra elk\n```\n\n| Subcommand | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `regenerate [--dry-run]` | Regenerate the 4 master config files from `.flisk/project.json` |\n| `verify [--json]` | Check generated files match `.flisk/project.json`. Drift exits 1 |\n| `migrate` | Migrate `service.json` files to the current schema version |\n| `edit` | Open `.flisk/project.json` in `$EDITOR` |\n| `enable-infra <service>` | Enable optional infrastructure: `monitoring`, `elk`, `debezium`, `golden_image`, `verdaccio` |\n| `disable-infra <service>` | Disable it again |\n\nSee [configuration](configuration.md), [environment, params and secrets](environment.md) and [generated files](generated-files.md).\n\n## 🩺 Diagnostics and tooling\n\n### `flisk doctor`\n\n```bash\nflisk doctor\nflisk doctor --no-ping                 # skip health pings\nflisk doctor --strict --json           # warnings fail, machine-readable\nflisk doctor --ping-timeout 2000\n```\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--no-ping` | Skip pinging running services' `/health` endpoints |\n| `--ping-timeout <ms>` | Per-service ping timeout (default 5000) |\n| `--fix` | Remove this project's unused Docker networks and leftover doctor probes (asks first) |\n| `--dry-run` | With `--fix`, print the commands without running them |\n| `-y, --yes` | With `--fix`, do not ask before each repair |\n| `--strict` | Fail on warnings, such as a WSL project under `/mnt/c` |\n| `--json` | Versioned JSON readiness report |\n\nSee the [doctor contract](reference/doctor-contract.md) and [troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md).\n\n### `flisk runtime`\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--check-assets` | Check the bundled engine and template assets |\n| `--json` | Print the result as JSON |\n\n### `flisk ui` (alias `interactive`)\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--no-animations` | Disable animations |\n| `--high-contrast` | High contrast mode |\n| `-v`, `--verbose` | More output |\n\nSee [UI](ui.md).\n\n### `flisk mcp`\n\nRuns a Model Context Protocol server over stdio for coding agents. No flags. See [agent hosts](agent-hosts.md).\n\n### `flisk completion [shell]`\n\nGenerates tab completion for `bash`, `zsh`, `fish` and `powershell`. Without a shell argument, `flisk completion` uses the shell named in `$SHELL` when it is one of these four, and `bash` otherwise.\n\nThe script is generated from the CLI itself: the command names, their flags, the resource types and the framework and language choices come from the registered commands and the registries, so the four shells always suggest the same commands and flags.\n\n| Flag | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `[shell]`, `-s`, `--shell <shell>` | `bash`, `zsh`, `fish`, `powershell` |\n| `-o`, `--output <path>` | Write the script to a file instead of stdout |\n| `--install` | Write the script to the shell's completion folder (PowerShell: add it to your profile) and print the line to add to your shell config |\n\n`--install` does not edit `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc`. Run the shell's setup below, then open a new terminal.\n\n#### What the completions suggest\n\n- Every command the CLI registers, with its aliases (`flisk ls` for `flisk stacks`, `flisk traefik` for `flisk networks`).\n- The sub-commands of a command, for example `flisk config <TAB>` (`regenerate`, `verify`, `edit`, ...).\n- The flags of the command you are on, for example `flisk doctor --<TAB>`.\n- The values of `--type`: the types `flisk resource` creates (`backend`, `frontend`, `worker`, `mcp`, `agent`, `bring-your-own`, `sdk`) and, for `flisk resources`, every type a resource can have (`library` and `migrator` as well).\n- The values of `--framework` and `--language`: the frameworks of the `--type` of the command line (the frontend frameworks for `--type frontend`, the backend frameworks otherwise) and the backend languages. A type that accepts neither offers nothing, because the CLI rejects the flag for it.\n- The stacks and the resources of the current project, for `flisk up`, `flisk stack`, `flisk logs`, `flisk restart` and `flisk agent sessions`, and behind the `--stack` and `--only` flags.\n\n#### bash\n\n```bash\nflisk completion --shell bash --install\necho 'source ~/.bash_completion.d/flisk-completion.bash' >> ~/.bashrc\nsource ~/.bashrc\n```\n\nThe first command writes `~/.bash_completion.d/flisk-completion.bash`. The second makes new shells load it.\n\n#### zsh\n\n```bash\nflisk completion --shell zsh --install\ncat >> ~/.zshrc <<'EOF'\nfpath+=(~/.zsh/completions)\nautoload -U compinit && compinit\nEOF\nsource ~/.zshrc\n```\n\nThe first command writes `~/.zsh/completions/_flisk`. The `fpath` line has to come before `compinit`. If your `~/.zshrc` already calls `compinit`, add only the `fpath+=(...)` line above it.\n\n#### fish\n\n```fish\nflisk completion --shell fish --install\n```\n\nThis writes `~/.config/fish/completions/flisk.fish`, which fish loads by itself. Open a new fish session.\n\n#### PowerShell\n\n```powershell\nflisk completion --shell powershell --install\n```\n\nThis adds a block marked `# FLISK-CLI-COMPLETION-START` / `# FLISK-CLI-COMPLETION-END` to your PowerShell profile (`Documents\\PowerShell` for PowerShell 7, `Documents\\WindowsPowerShell` otherwise). Close and reopen PowerShell. PowerShell suggests the command names, the sub-commands, the flags of the current command, the values of `--type`, `--framework` and `--language` and the stacks and the resources of the current project.\n\n#### Check that it works\n\nType `flisk do` and press Tab. The shell should offer `doctor` and `down`. If nothing happens, check that you opened a new terminal after the setup above.\n\n#### After you upgrade\n\nThe completions are a copy of the CLI: a new command, a new flag or a new framework shows up only after the script is regenerated. Run the same `--install` command again after `flisk upgrade`:\n\n```bash\nflisk completion --shell bash --install   # or zsh, fish, powershell\n```\n\nIt overwrites the old file (PowerShell: it replaces the marked block), so the completions match the new version. Without `--install`, `flisk completion <shell> > <file>` or `--output <file>` writes the script wherever you want it, and you load it yourself.\n\n### `flisk maintainers check [file]`\n\nChecks that `MAINTAINERS.md` (or `[file]`) lists 3 maintainers from 2 companies.\n\n## 🤖 JSON output and exit codes\n\nCommands with `--json`: `projects`, `resources`, `stacks`, `status`, `up`, `down`, `logs`, `networks`, `doctor`, `runtime`, `config verify`, `config explain`.\n\n| Exit code | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| 0 | Success (doctor allows warnings unless `--strict`) |\n| 1 | Blocking finding or command failure |\n| 2 | Invalid arguments or an unexpected internal error |\n\nstdout holds one JSON document; diagnostics go to stderr. See [machine-readable CLI](reference/machine-readable-cli.md).\n\n## 🌱 Environment variables\n\n| Variable | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `FLISK_HTTP_PORT`, `FLISK_HTTPS_PORT`, `FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT` | Override the host ports Flisk picks |\n| `FLISK_BIND_ADDRESS` | Bind address for dev ports (default `127.0.0.1`; `0.0.0.0` to test from a phone) |\n| `TILT_PORT` | Tilt UI port (default 10350) |\n| `FLISK_SERVICE_BASE_URL` | Base URL for doctor pings and `up` smoke checks (default `<project>.localhost`) |\n| `FLISK_UP_READY_TIMEOUT_MS` | How long `flisk up --json` waits for readiness (default 15 minutes) |\n| `FLISK_ALLOW_NATIVE_WINDOWS` | Set to `1` to allow `flisk up` on native Windows |\n| `FLISK_LICENSE_KEY` | Unlocks premium features such as `--ddd` |\n| `FLISK_DEBUG` | Set to `1` with `flisk up --verbose` to include Starlark debug logs |\n\nProject `.env` keys (`DB_PASSWORD`, `DATABASE_URL`, `JWT_SECRET`, ...) are covered in [environment, params and secrets](environment.md).\n\n## 📁 Files Flisk writes\n\n| Path | What it is |\n| --- | --- |\n| `.flisk/project.json` | Project settings. `flisk config regenerate` rebuilds from it |\n| `.flisk/.flisk-out/` | The 4 master files: `Tiltfile`, `spec.master`, `TILT_RESOURCE_DEFAULTS.star`, `TILT_TECH_STACK.star` |\n| `services/<group>/<name>/service.json` | One per resource: type, port, stack, params, secrets. **Edit this** |\n| `services/<group>/<name>/.autogenerated/` | Docker and Tilt files generated from `service.json`. Don't hand-edit them |\n| `.env` | Generated credentials. Gitignored |\n| `shared-platform-engineering/` | Shared Docker helper scripts |\n\nSee [generated files](generated-files.md) and [layout](layout.md).\n\n## 🍳 Recipes\n\n```bash\n# CI gate\nflisk projects --json --check && flisk config verify --json && flisk doctor --no-ping --json\n\n# Start one service and what it needs, in the background, for an agent\nflisk up --only orders-api --json > up.json &\n\n# What is running and where?\nflisk status --tilt && flisk networks --raw\n\n# Last 5 minutes of logs from one service, as JSON\nflisk logs -s orders-api --since 5m --json\n\n# Clean shutdown, including unused networks\nflisk down --prune-networks\n```\n\nMore in [recipes](recipes/) and [CI](ci.md). Found a missing flag? [Open an issue](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/issues/new). 🙌\n",
  "configuration.md": "# Configuration and editor schemas\n\nFlisk has two JSON files with different jobs: each service's `service.json` describes that service, and `.flisk/project.json` configures the local project and its stack phases. The schemas help editors provide completion; Flisk's CLI behavior remains authoritative.\n\n## `service.json`\n\nHere is valid JSON; the explanations are outside the file so it remains parseable:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://flisk.sh/schema.service.json\",\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"appName\": \"api-backend\",\n  \"appType\": \"backend\",\n  \"stack\": \"shop\",\n  \"port\": 4000,\n  \"healthCheckPath\": \"/health\",\n  \"dependsOn\": []\n}\n```\n\n`appName`, `appType`, `stack`, and `schemaVersion` are required. A backend also needs a port in the supported backend range. See the [authoritative service schema](../runtime/engine/schemas/service-schema.json). The `$schema` property is supported by the schema. `flisk doctor` checks local environment readiness and discovered service issues.\n\n### `smoke`: check the public URL after `flisk up`\n\n`healthCheckPath` is a GET inside the container, and `traefik.healthCheck` is Traefik's own probe. Neither opens the URL `flisk up` prints, so a container can be green while the route is a 404, the router is shared with another backend, or the database is not the one the service uses. A `smoke` block closes that gap:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"smoke\": {\n    \"via\": \"proxy\",\n    \"timeoutSeconds\": 60,\n    \"steps\": [\n      { \"name\": \"create\", \"method\": \"POST\", \"path\": \"/records\", \"body\": { \"name\": \"smoke\" }, \"expect\": 201, \"save\": { \"id\": \"$.id\" } },\n      { \"name\": \"read back\", \"path\": \"/records/{{id}}\", \"expect\": 200, \"bodyContains\": \"smoke\" }\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- Once Tilt is up, `flisk up` sends each step to the service's public base URL (`http://api.<project>.localhost[:port]/api/<name>` for a backend, `mcp` or bring-your-own service, `http://app.<project>.localhost[:port]/<name>` for a frontend), the same URL `flisk up` prints. `path` must start with `/`; a full URL or relative path is rejected, because it would skip the proxy.\n- `expect` is the status (default 200). `bodyContains` is optional. `save` copies a response field (`$.id`, `$.data.items[0].id`) into `{{id}}` for later steps, in the path and in string values of `body`.\n- Before the first step, `flisk up` polls `GET <public URL><healthCheckPath>` (default `/health` for backend and `mcp` services; none for frontends and bring-your-own without `healthCheckPath`, which is the setting for a service that is not HTTP) until something other than a connection error, 404 or a 5xx answers, so a write is never the first request to reach a service that is still building or migrating. The wait has its own `timeoutSeconds` budget, and the steps then get a fresh one. If the service never answers, the first step runs anyway and reports the failure.\n- A 404, 502, 503 or 504 means the route is not up yet and is retried for `timeoutSeconds` (default 60). A thrown error is retried for GET, HEAD and OPTIONS; for a write (POST, PUT, ...) only when the error proves the request was never sent (connection refused, DNS failure), never after a timeout or reset, which may have reached the service. Any other wrong status fails at once, so a write is never repeated.\n- A failed step stops Tilt, prints the service, step, URL, method, status and a body snippet, and makes `flisk up` exit non-zero. The containers are left running for inspection; `flisk down` removes them.\n- Every step that ran leaves a record under `.flisk/smoke/<service>/<step-slug>/` (`<step-slug>` is the lowercased step name, or `step-N` when unnamed). `latest.json` holds the service, step, method, public URL, expected and actual status, `truncated`, the byte length and the body file name; `body.txt` holds the response body, capped at 64 KiB (`truncated: true` when the cap cut it). A step that got no response (after retries) writes `latest.json` with `status: null` and the error code, and no body file. When a step passes, `last-success.json` and `last-success-body.txt` are replaced with that response; a failure never touches them, so a later regression can be compared with the last good response (`diff last-success-body.txt body.txt`). The failure line ends with `(record: <path to latest.json>)`. Before the first record is written, `flisk up` makes sure the project `.gitignore` ignores `.flisk/smoke/`, and warns when it cannot; Git ignore does not untrack files that were added before. `flisk down` leaves the records, and nothing is uploaded from your machine; `rm -rf .flisk/smoke/` removes them. Bodies are masked before they are stored or shown: the secret values of the project's `.env` are replaced with `[REDACTED]` in the stored body, in the last-success copy, in the printed snippet, and in the recorded URL and error. That is not a secret scanner: it covers the values the `.env` holds and nothing else, and a project without a `.env` has nothing to mask. When the `.env` exists but cannot be read, the values are unknown, so the runner withholds the body and the URL and keeps only the safe metadata instead of storing unmasked text. Request headers and cookies are not stored.\n- No `smoke` block, no check: existing projects behave as before. Workers and bring-your-own services with `exposeViaProxy: false` have no public route and are skipped. `flisk doctor` reports a malformed block, and `flisk up` reports one as a failed smoke check instead of crashing.\n\nNot covered: a browser test (HTTP only), proving which database answered (a read-back of the written id catches a wrong store, not which one), a Traefik `pathPrefix` override (the check uses the `apiPath` or `basePath` route that `flisk up` prints), and `--dry-run` (nothing is started, so nothing is checked). The ingress port is the one `flisk up` selected (`FLISK_HTTP_PORT` or the fallback range).\n\n`tests/e2e/journeys/smoke/verify-smoke.sh` checks both outcomes through a real `flisk up`.\n\nA short drawing of the pass and fail paths: [Smoke check](smoke.md).\n\n## `.flisk/project.json`\n\n`flisk project` creates this file. Here is a minimal valid one; the explanations are outside the file so it remains parseable:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"version\": \"1.0\",\n  \"project\": {\n    \"name\": \"shop-app\",\n    \"version\": \"1.0.0\"\n  },\n  \"phases\": {\n    \"pre_alpha\": {\n      \"name\": \"Pre-Alpha\",\n      \"description\": \"Core services\",\n      \"enabledStacks\": [\"shop\"]\n    },\n    \"alpha\": {\n      \"name\": \"Alpha\",\n      \"description\": \"Essential business services\",\n      \"enabledStacks\": []\n    },\n    \"beta\": {\n      \"name\": \"Beta\",\n      \"description\": \"Extended features\",\n      \"enabledStacks\": []\n    },\n    \"out_of_scope\": {\n      \"name\": \"Out of Scope\",\n      \"description\": \"Future releases\",\n      \"enabledStacks\": []\n    }\n  },\n  \"optional_infra\": {\n    \"monitoring\": false,\n    \"elk\": false,\n    \"debezium\": false,\n    \"golden_image\": true,\n    \"verdaccio\": false\n  },\n  \"discovery\": {\n    \"paths\": [\"services/*/*\"]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n- `project`: the project name and version.\n- `phases`: all four phases (`pre_alpha`, `alpha`, `beta`, `out_of_scope`) are required, and `enabledStacks` lists the stacks assigned to each phase.\n- `optional_infra`: switches for optional platform services such as monitoring and the golden image.\n- `discovery.paths`: where Flisk looks for each service's `service.json`. `services/*/*` matches `services/<stack>/<resource>/service.json`.\n\nThe schema only gives your editor completion and hints. The CLI stays the source of truth when it reads this file at runtime.\n\nFor VS Code, associate both filenames with their schema in workspace settings. The project schema provides editor assistance for the current project configuration shape; it does not add or change CLI validation.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"json.schemas\": [\n    {\n      \"fileMatch\": [\"/services/**/service.json\"],\n      \"url\": \"https://flisk.sh/schema.service.json\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"fileMatch\": [\"/.flisk/project.json\"],\n      \"url\": \"https://flisk.sh/project-schema.json\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe service schema is also in [`runtime/engine/schemas/service-schema.json`](../runtime/engine/schemas/service-schema.json), and the current project configuration schema is [`runtime/engine/schemas/project-schema.json`](../runtime/engine/schemas/project-schema.json). Run `flisk config verify` to check generated project files, validate discovered service manifests, and compare generated service files with their snapshots. It does not validate Helm values. `flisk doctor` checks local readiness and service concerns. See [generated service files](generated-files.md).\n\n### Which diagnostic command should I run?\n\nUse `flisk doctor` to check whether your development environment is ready. Use `flisk config verify` to validate project configuration and check generated-file consistency. These commands answer different questions and are not substitutes for each other.\n\n| What you want to check | Command | What it helps identify |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Local environment readiness | `flisk doctor` | Missing tools, Docker readiness, port conflicts, and reported service or environment issues |\n| Project configuration and generated-file consistency | `flisk config verify` | Invalid service manifests, missing or out-of-sync generated project files, and generated service-file drift |\n\n#### Example: Check environment readiness\n\nIf Flisk cannot start your development environment, run:\n\n```sh\nflisk doctor\n```\n\nReview the reported checks and suggested fixes. For example, if Docker is not running, start Docker Desktop or another supported container runtime and run the command again.\n\nSee the [doctor readiness contract](reference/doctor-contract.md) for the exact checks, exit codes, and JSON output.\n\n#### Example: Check configuration consistency\n\nIf you suspect your configuration or generated files are out of sync, run:\n\n```sh\nflisk config verify\n```\n\nReview any validation errors or reported differences. For example, if a generated project file has been edited manually, verification can report that it is out of sync and show a diff. Use the reported remediation guidance to address the problem; verification does not automatically regenerate files.\n\nFor example, verification may report an out-of-sync generated file and recommend running:\n\n```sh\nflisk config regenerate\n```\n\nRegenerate only after reviewing the reported differences and confirming that regeneration is appropriate for your changes.\n\nSee the [machine-readable CLI reference](reference/machine-readable-cli.md) for the verification result fields and exit behavior. For generated-file drift and regeneration guidance, see [generated service files](generated-files.md).\n\n### The service schema and how it is published\n\n`runtime/engine/schemas/service-schema.json` is the source of truth; the copy at `https://flisk.sh/schema.service.json` is a\ndeploy artifact for editors. It is only an editor hint. The CLI provider registries are the contract: the CLI rejects an unknown\n`framework` or `language` id before it writes a resource. So `framework` and `language` are open strings with an id pattern, their\n`examples` are a short hint rather than a list of every provider, and adding a provider never changes the schema.\n\n- **On a pull request**, CI validates the schema offline (`scripts/check-published-service-schema.mjs`). It never fetches the live\n  copy, because a PR that changes the schema could never match it before merge.\n- **After a change to the schema lands on `main`**, the *Publish service schema* workflow copies it to the Pages repository and then\n  reports in its run summary whether the live copy matches. It needs a repository secret named `PAGES_SYNC_TOKEN` (a fine-grained\n  token with `contents: write` on `flisk-sh/flisk-sh.github.io`). Without it the copy is skipped with a warning and the live\n  copy falls behind `main`; the summary says so.\n",
  "data.md": "# Local data: where it lives, how to reset, seed and snapshot\n\nHow the shared Postgres behaves under Flisk. Every command below was run against a real `postgres:16-alpine` container named the way Flisk names it. `flisk up` itself was not run for this page.\n\n## Where the data lives\n\nFlisk runs **one** Postgres container for the whole project. The generated `DATABASE_URL` of a service points at a database named after the project and the **stack**, so the services of one stack share a database.\n\n| Thing | Name | Example for project `my-shop`, stack `store` |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Container | `<project>_postgres` | `my_shop_postgres` |\n| Superuser | `<project>` | `my_shop` |\n| Password | `DB_PASSWORD` in the project `.env` | |\n| Database | `<project>_<stack>` (read from the generated `DATABASE_URL`) | `my_shop_store` |\n| Volume | `database-management_<project>_postgres_data` | `database-management_my_shop_postgres_data` |\n| Host port | `15432`, or `FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT` | |\n\n`<project>` is `project.name` from `.flisk/project.json` with `-` replaced by `_`. The same rule applies to the stack name. The volume name was read from existing volumes on a machine that had run Flisk (for example `database-management_auth_user_postgres_data` for project `auth-user`), so check yours with `docker volume ls | grep postgres_data`. The database name was read from the generated Compose `DATABASE_URL` (for project `pilot`, stack `shop`: `.../pilot_shop`), not from a running container: list the real ones with `docker exec <container> psql -U <user> -d postgres -c '\\l'`.\n\n## Does it survive `flisk down`?\n\nYes. `flisk down` runs `tilt down`, which removes the containers but not named volumes. Checked with a small Compose project run through `tilt down`: the container was removed and the file in the volume was still there afterwards. The next `flisk up` starts Postgres on the same volume. Since [#585](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/pull/585) `flisk down` also stops this project's `tilt up`, and `flisk down --prune-networks` removes the project's Docker networks that no container uses; by the source it touches networks only, not volumes (not run against Docker here). Without that flag the networks are kept.\n\n## Start from a clean state\n\nEverything, all services:\n\n```bash\nflisk down\ndocker volume rm database-management_my_shop_postgres_data\nflisk up\n```\n\nOne stack's database (Postgres must be running; `WITH (FORCE)` disconnects the service first):\n\n```bash\ndocker exec my_shop_postgres psql -U my_shop -d postgres -c \"DROP DATABASE my_shop_store WITH (FORCE)\"\ndocker exec my_shop_postgres createdb -U my_shop -O my_shop my_shop_store\n```\n\nThe database comes back empty. Migrations run again only when your migrator runs again (`flisk up`, or restart the migrator resource in Tilt).\n\n## Seed\n\nPick one:\n\n- **A SQL file, by hand.** Fast and fine for a pilot:\n\n  ```bash\n  docker exec -i my_shop_postgres psql -U my_shop -d my_shop_store -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 < seed.sql\n  ```\n\n- **A one-shot resource.** Run the seed as a bring-your-own job with `--restart no`, the same way as a migration; see [One-shot jobs](byo.md#one-shot-jobs-migrations-seeders). A dbmate job was checked through a real `flisk up`. That a seed job runs after the migrator and before the API is **not checked here**; make the seed idempotent so a different order is harmless.\n\nKeep seed data fake. Do not put production data in the repository.\n\n### Worked example: a seed job\n\nThis is a bring-your-own job that loads two fake rows and exits. It follows the [one-shot job pattern](byo.md#one-shot-jobs-migrations-seeders), and [`examples/byo-jobs/atlas`](../examples/byo-jobs/atlas/README.md) is the checked job it is modelled on. Put these four files in `services/store/seed-demo/`.\n\n`seed.sql` creates a small table if it is missing, then inserts fake rows and skips any that already exist, so running it again changes nothing. In a real project your migrator creates the table; then delete the `create table` statement.\n\n```sql\n-- Fake data only. Safe to run again: the table is created if missing and existing rows are skipped.\ncreate table if not exists demo_customers (\n  id integer primary key,\n  name text not null,\n  email text not null unique\n);\n\ninsert into demo_customers (id, name, email) values\n  (1, 'Test Customer One', 'customer1@example.test'),\n  (2, 'Test Customer Two', 'customer2@example.test')\non conflict do nothing;\n```\n\n`seed.sh` reads the `DATABASE_URL` that Flisk passes to the job and refuses a database name that has anything other than letters, digits and underscores (Flisk's names only use those), so the name is safe to put in SQL. It then waits for Postgres, for at most 30 one-second tries (set `SEED_WAIT_TRIES` to change that), and exits with the last connection error if Postgres never answers. Then it creates the database if it is missing (Flisk does not create it for a bring-your-own job) and runs `seed.sql`:\n\n```sh\n#!/bin/sh\nset -eu\nurl=\"${DATABASE_URL%%\\?*}\"   # drop the Prisma-style ?schema=public that psql rejects\ndb=\"${url##*/}\"\nserver=\"${url%/*}\"\ncase \"$db\" in\n  \"\"|*[!A-Za-z0-9_]*) echo \"seed: database name '$db' must use only letters, digits and underscores\" >&2; exit 1 ;;\nesac\nexport PGSSLMODE=disable     # Flisk's Postgres has SSL off\ntries=0\nmax=\"${SEED_WAIT_TRIES:-30}\"\nuntil err=$(psql \"$server/postgres\" -tAc \"select 1\" 2>&1 >/dev/null); do\n  tries=$((tries + 1))\n  if [ \"$tries\" -ge \"$max\" ]; then echo \"seed: Postgres not ready after $max tries: $err\" >&2; exit 1; fi\n  sleep 1\ndone\npsql \"$server/postgres\" -tAc \"select 1 from pg_database where datname = '$db'\" | grep -q 1 \\\n  || psql \"$server/postgres\" -c \"create database \\\"$db\\\"\"\nexec psql \"$url\" -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -f /seed.sql\n```\n\n`Dockerfile` installs `psql` and runs the script:\n\n```dockerfile\nFROM alpine:3.20@sha256:d9e853e87e55526f6b2917df91a2115c36dd7c696a35be12163d44e6e2a4b6bc\nRUN apk add --no-cache postgresql-client\nCOPY seed.sql seed.sh /\nRUN addgroup -g 1000 app && adduser -u 1000 -G app -D -H app\nUSER app\nHEALTHCHECK NONE\nCMD [\"sh\", \"/seed.sh\"]\n```\n\nRegister it from that folder. `--restart no` is what makes it a one-shot job:\n\n```bash\nflisk resource seed-demo --type bring-your-own --stack store --dockerfile ./Dockerfile --restart no --register-existing --yes\n```\n\nThat writes the `service.json` below. Add the `dependsOn` line yourself so the job waits for the shared Postgres; if a migrator creates your tables, list the migrator instead (a migrator already depends on `postgres`). The job does not listen on `port`, so `healthCheckPath` is not used.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"$schema\": \"https://flisk.sh/schema.service.json\",\n  \"schemaVersion\": 1,\n  \"appName\": \"seed-demo\",\n  \"appType\": \"bring-your-own\",\n  \"stack\": \"store\",\n  \"port\": 4000,\n  \"healthCheckPath\": \"/health\",\n  \"dockerfile\": \"./Dockerfile\",\n  \"restart\": \"no\",\n  \"dependsOn\": [\"postgres\"]\n}\n```\n\nRestart behaviour: with `\"restart\": \"no\"` Docker does not start the container again after it exits `0`. Without it the default is `unless-stopped`, and the finished job would be started again and again. To run the seed a second time, restart the `seed-demo` resource in Tilt.\n\nRun it and check the rows (names for project `my-shop`, as in the tables above):\n\n```bash\nflisk up store\ndocker ps -a --filter name=seed-demo --format '{{.Names}}\\t{{.Status}}'      # expect \"Exited (0)\"\ndocker exec my_shop_postgres psql -U my_shop -d my_shop_store -c \"select id, name from demo_customers order by id\"\n```\n\nYou should see the two `Test Customer` rows. After restarting the resource, the same two rows remain.\n\nNot checked here: running this through a real `flisk up` (no Docker or Tilt available). The checked parts are `seed.sql` against a real Postgres (the second run leaves the same two rows), the shell syntax and URL handling of `seed.sh`, and the manifest against the service schema and `flisk config verify`. The Dockerfile and the `psql` calls follow the checked Atlas job.\n\n## Snapshot and restore\n\n```bash\n# snapshot one database\ndocker exec my_shop_postgres pg_dump -U my_shop -Fc my_shop_store > store.dump\n\n# restore it over the current state\ndocker exec -i my_shop_postgres pg_restore -U my_shop -d my_shop_store --clean --if-exists --no-owner < store.dump\n```\n\nBoth were run: after a restore, a row inserted after the snapshot was gone. A shared team snapshot is just that file; Flisk has no command for it, and no tooling to scrub production data. Anonymise a dump before it leaves production.\n\n## Not covered\n\n- Other databases (MySQL, Mongo, Turso): only the shared Postgres was checked.\n- A built-in `flisk db reset` or `flisk db seed` command does not exist.\n- The start-up order between migrator, seed and API. Related, and still open: [#531](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/issues/531) (an explicit, validated `dependsOn` on Postgres).\n",
  "troubleshooting.md": "# Troubleshooting: flisk and Tilt messages and what to do about them\n\nSearch this page for the text you saw. Each entry gives the message as Flisk prints it, the cause, the fix, and where in the repository the message is defined. Every entry also says how it was checked:\n\n- **Reproduced:** the output below was captured by running Flisk 1.3.86 (`origin/main` at the time of writing) in a scratch project on macOS. The scratch project's directory is shown as `<project>`. `flisk up` was **not** run for this page, so nothing here was observed while Docker was starting containers.\n- **Simulated:** produced by running the real `flisk` against a stand-in for Docker (a small wrapper that makes `docker` fail the way the entry describes). The message and fix are Flisk's own; the Docker failure itself was not real.\n- **Read from source, not reproduced:** copied from the code. It is the intended text; whether your situation produces it was not checked.\n\nWhere the cause is not known, the entry says so. Messages change between releases: if yours differs slightly, search for the first few words.\n\nFixes that change files or Docker state, such as `flisk down --prune-networks` or `docker network prune`, affect every project on that Docker daemon, not only the one you are in.\n\nOn this page: [before anything starts](#flisk-fails-before-anything-starts) · [doctor failures](#flisk-doctor-fails) · [ports](#ports) · [services not healthy](#services-never-turn-healthy-or-are-left-behind) · [scaffolded code](#scaffolded-code) · [Windows and WSL2](#windows-and-wsl2) · [asking for help](#how-to-ask-for-help)\n\n## flisk fails before anything starts\n\n### `Could not find project root`\n\n```text\n$ flisk status        # also: flisk up\nError: Could not find project root (no .flisk/project.json found). Run `flisk project --yes` first.\n```\n\n- **Cause:** you ran the command outside a Flisk project. Flisk looks upward from the current directory for `.flisk/project.json`.\n- **Fix:** `cd` into the project, or run `flisk project --yes` to create one.\n- **Defined in:** [`errors.ts`](../cli/src/utils/errors.ts) (`requireProjectRoot`, `errorFactories.notInProject`). Some commands print the second wording from `errorFactories`, `❌ Could not find project root (no .flisk/project.json found)`, followed by suggestions.\n- **Checked:** reproduced (`flisk status` and `flisk up`, exit code 1).\n\n### `unknown option` and `unknown command`\n\n```text\n$ flisk resource x --bogus\nerror: unknown option '--bogus'\n$ flisk upp\nerror: unknown command 'upp'\n(Did you mean up?)\n```\n\n- **Cause:** a typo, or a flag the command does not have. The exit code is 1.\n- **Fix:** `flisk <command> --help` lists the flags.\n- **Checked:** reproduced.\n- If an older Flisk reports `unknown flag: --force`, upgrade it. Current Flisk accepts the option for script compatibility and does not pass it to Tilt. The historical [pilot report](adopt-flisk.md#2-pilot-in-one-afternoon) ran `tilt down --force` directly, not `flisk down --force`.\n\n### `Stack \"x\" not found`\n\n```text\n$ flisk up nope --dry-run\n❌ Stack \"nope\" not found\n\n💡 Suggestions:\n   → Run `flisk stacks` to see available stacks\n   → Run `flisk stack` to assign resources to a stack\n```\n\n- **Cause:** no resource is assigned to a stack of that name. Stacks are the `<stack>` folder in `services/<stack>/<name>/`.\n- **Fix:** `flisk stacks` lists what Flisk found.\n- **Defined in:** [`errors.ts`](../cli/src/utils/errors.ts) (`stackNotFound`). `Resource \"x\" not found` is the same pattern.\n- **Checked:** reproduced for the stack message; the resource message was read from source, not reproduced.\n\n### `flisk X is older than the Y this project requires (minFliskVersion)`\n\n```text\n$ flisk up shop --dry-run        # same text from flisk up and flisk doctor\nflisk 1.3.86 is older than the 9.9.9 this project requires (minFliskVersion)\nRun: flisk upgrade\n```\n\nWith `\"minFliskVersion\": \"abc\"` in `.flisk/project.json`:\n\n```text\n.flisk/project.json minFliskVersion \"abc\" is not a version like \"1.3.80\"\nSet minFliskVersion to a MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH string, for example \"1.3.80\".\n```\n\n- **Cause:** the repository pins a minimum CLI version and yours is older, or the pin is not `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. Both stop `flisk up` with exit code 1, before Docker or Tilt are touched.\n- **Fix:** `flisk upgrade`, or install a specific version; see [upgrading](upgrading.md). `flisk up --ignore-version` skips the check, which is only sensible when the pin is stale.\n- **Defined in:** [`flisk-version.ts`](../cli/src/utils/flisk-version.ts).\n- **Checked:** reproduced with a scratch `minFliskVersion` of `9.9.9` and `abc`.\n\n### `Cold start blocked` (Docker or Tilt missing)\n\n`flisk up` checks the machine before anything else. With a Docker that cannot be reached (simulated with a wrapper that answers like a stopped daemon):\n\n```text\n$ flisk up shop\nCannot connect to the Docker daemon at unix:///var/run/docker.sock. Is the docker daemon running?\nCold start blocked. Bun/Prisma/NATS are not the first failure.\nThey are generated after `flisk project`. Fix the machine checks below.\nFAIL docker  Docker daemon is not running\n  Start Docker Desktop, OrbStack, or Colima, then retry: flisk doctor\nFAIL compose Docker Compose plugin not found\n  Install Docker Compose: https://docs.docker.com/compose/install/\n```\n\nWith `tilt` removed from `PATH` (reproduced):\n\n```text\nCold start blocked. Bun/Prisma/NATS are not the first failure.\nThey are generated after `flisk project`. Fix the machine checks below.\nFAIL tilt    Tilt CLI not found\n  curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tilt-dev/tilt/master/scripts/install.sh | bash\n```\n\n- **Cause:** no Docker daemon, no Compose plugin, or no Tilt on `PATH`. The Compose line appears with the daemon line here because the stand-in made every `docker` call fail; with a real stopped daemon, whether it also prints is not checked.\n- **Fix:** start Docker Desktop, OrbStack or Colima, or install Tilt, then `flisk doctor`.\n- **Defined in:** [`cold-preflight.ts`](../cli/src/utils/cold-preflight.ts) and `DOCTOR_FIXES` in [`doctor.ts`](../cli/src/commands/doctor.ts).\n- **Checked:** Tilt missing reproduced; Docker down simulated. Other texts in the same family, **read from source, not reproduced**, from [`errors.ts`](../cli/src/utils/errors.ts): `Tilt CLI is not installed`, `Docker (or a compatible container runtime) is not running`, `Docker is running but not responding (`docker ps` hung for 10s)` (fix: quit and reopen Docker Desktop, or `colima restart`), all under `Flisk can't start - missing prerequisites:`.\n- **Only `flisk up` is blocked:** `flisk project` writes the project configuration and clones a starter template without a Docker daemon or Tilt, and prints the next step instead. `flisk doctor` remains the explicit readiness check.\n- **See also:** [Troubleshoot L1 (base image) build failures](troubleshoot-l1.md) for failures pulling the upstream base image, Alpine package installation errors, or missing golden layers.\n- **See also:** [Troubleshoot L2 (dependency layer) build failures](troubleshoot-l2.md) for package installation, lockfile synchronization, and Docker layer caching issues.\n\n### `Out of sync: ...` from `flisk config verify`\n\n```text\n$ flisk config verify\n🔍 Verifying configuration...\n\n⚠️  Configuration issues found:\n   - Out of sync: .flisk/.flisk-out/TILT_TECH_STACK.star (run 'flisk config regenerate')\n   - Out of sync: .tiltignore (run 'flisk config regenerate')\n\n--- a/.flisk/.flisk-out/TILT_TECH_STACK.star\n+++ b/.flisk/.flisk-out/TILT_TECH_STACK.star\n...\nRun `flisk config regenerate` to fix.\n```\n\n(The diff shows the whole file as changed, with the same text on both sides; only the line you edited differs. This output came from appending one line to each file.)\n\n- **Cause:** a generated file was edited by hand, or `.flisk/project.json` changed since the files were generated. The message appears with the same text for a `.tiltignore` that Flisk wrote and someone edited ([gradual adoption](gradual-adoption.md#your-own-tiltignore) covers a `.tiltignore` Flisk did not write).\n- **Fix:** `flisk config regenerate`. It restores a root `.tiltignore` that still carries Flisk's `SYSTEM-GENERATED BY Flisk CLI` header and prints `Restored: .tiltignore → project root (hand edits discarded)`; your edit is gone, so keep a copy if you want it. A `.tiltignore` without that header is never overwritten (it is the team's own). `flisk project --yes` does not restore it. Before this was fixed, `flisk config regenerate` left the edited file as it was and the only way out was to delete the root `.tiltignore` and run `flisk config regenerate` again.\n- **Defined in:** [`template-engine.ts`](../cli/src/generator/template-engine.ts) and [`config.ts`](../cli/src/commands/config.ts).\n- **Checked:** reproduced, including the failing fix and the workaround.\n\n## flisk doctor fails\n\n`flisk doctor` prints `✗` for a failure, `○` for a skipped check and `ℹ Fix:` under a failure. It exits 1 when any check fails. The JSON form is described in the [doctor contract](reference/doctor-contract.md). The scratch outputs below used `flisk doctor --no-ping`, which does not call running services.\n\n### Docker and Tilt\n\nAll **read from source, not reproduced** except where stated. Defined in [`doctor.ts`](../cli/src/commands/doctor.ts) unless noted.\n\n| Message | Fix as printed |\n| --- | --- |\n| `Docker is not running` / `Docker daemon is not running` (also reproduced through a stand-in, see above) | Start Docker Desktop, OrbStack, or Colima, then retry: `flisk doctor` |\n| `Docker daemon is not responding` | Same fix text |\n| `Colima is installed but not running` | Same fix text |\n| `Docker is using the <os> engine; Flisk requires Linux containers` | Switch Docker Desktop to Linux containers, enable WSL2 integration, then retry `flisk doctor` |\n| `Docker is too old for generated healthchecks (they use start_interval)`, followed by the versions found | Update Docker Desktop, or on Linux update `docker-ce` and the `docker-compose-plugin` package. The README states Engine 25+ and Compose 2.20+ |\n| `Tilt CLI not found` (reproduced) | The Tilt install script, as in the entry above |\n| `Tilt <version> is below the required v0.25.0 floor` | Install Tilt v0.25.0 or newer: https://docs.tilt.dev/install.html |\n| `Bun is not available` / `Bun <version> is below the required 1.2.0 floor` | `curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install \\| bash`, or install Bun 1.2.0 or newer. Checked only when the project has generated JavaScript services |\n\nPodman: doctor prints `Podman is running` when it is the runtime it finds, and `Docker daemon is not running` otherwise. Whether a Podman setup can run a whole stack was not checked.\n\n### `Docker cannot create another network: all predefined address pools are in use`\n\n```text\n✗ Docker cannot create another network: all predefined address pools are in use. Each Flisk project needs several\nℹ Fix: run flisk down --prune-networks in projects you are not using, or docker network prune (removes all unused networks)\n```\n\n- **Cause:** Docker's default address pools are used up. Each Flisk project creates several networks and keeps them after `flisk down`. Docker's own error text is `all predefined address pools have been fully subnetted`; doctor matches `address pools`.\n- **Fix:** run `flisk down --prune-networks` in projects you no longer use, or `docker network prune`, which removes every unused network on that Docker daemon, whoever created it. `--prune-networks` was added in [#585](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/pull/585).\n- **If you skip doctor:** since [#589](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/pull/589), the `init-networks` resource in Tilt fails with `Failed to create Docker network <name>: <Docker's message>` and exits 1. Before it, the failure was swallowed (the command ended `|| true`), `init-networks` reported success, and the later failure was `network ... declared as external, but could not be found`, which is what [byo.md](byo.md#running-more-than-one-project) and the [moon recipe](recipes/moon.md) describe. The `Failed to create Docker network` text is in [`utils_docker_networks.star`](../runtime/engine/topologies/tilt/common/utils_docker_networks.star).\n- **Checked:** the doctor message is **simulated** (the stand-in `docker` failed `network create` with Docker's text). The `init-networks` and `declared as external` messages are **read from source and docs, not reproduced**; this machine's Docker pools are in use by other people's work and were not touched.\n\nNote that `flisk doctor` itself runs `docker network create flisk_doctor_probe_<pid>` and removes it, to test whether a network can be created.\n\n### Environment variables\n\n```text\n$ flisk doctor --no-ping      # no .env file\n✗ Missing required env variables: TILT_ENV, DB_PASSWORD\nℹ Fix: No .env file yet. Run `flisk project` to create one with working defaults.\n\n# .env exists but TILT_ENV was deleted from it\n✗ Missing required env variables: TILT_ENV\nℹ Fix: Edit .env and set these values:\n    TILT_ENV=<value>\n\n# DB_PASSWORD= is present but empty\n✗ Invalid env variables: DB_PASSWORD is set but empty\nℹ Fix: Edit .env and provide values for empty variables\n```\n\n- **Cause:** a required key is absent or empty in the project `.env`. See [environment](environment.md).\n- **Fix:** as printed. `flisk project` writes a `.env` with working defaults and does not need Docker.\n- **Defined in:** [`doctor.ts`](../cli/src/commands/doctor.ts) (`checkEnvironmentVariables`) and [`env-validator.ts`](../cli/src/utils/env-validator.ts).\n- **Checked:** reproduced, all three. When only a warning applies, doctor passes and appends `(warning: ...)`; for example a `DATABASE_URL` password that differs from `DB_PASSWORD` (read from source, not reproduced).\n\n### Project files that do not match what Flisk expects\n\n```text\n✗ 1 resource without a package.json: billing-api. The image build stops at \"COPY .../package.json: not found\"\nℹ Fix: Add a package.json next to each service.json (copy one from a working resource), or delete the folder's service.json if it is not a service\n\n✗ Services missing required package.json scripts:\n    orders-api: missing \"build\"\nℹ Fix: Add the missing scripts to each service's package.json, e.g.:\n    \"dev\": \"bun run src/index.ts\",\n    \"build\": \"tsc\",\n    \"start\": \"bun run dist/index.js\"\n\n✗ 1 resource outside discovery.paths [\"services/*/*\"]: other/deep/x/billing-api. `flisk up` will not start them\nℹ Fix: Move each under services/<stack>/<name>, or add a matching pattern to discovery.paths in .flisk/project.json and run: flisk config regenerate\n\n✗ Master configs missing: TILT_TECH_STACK.star\nℹ Fix: Run: flisk project\n```\n\n- **Cause and fix:** as printed. The last one also produces a second failure, `Starlark load() issues will stop Tilt before services start:`, listing the missing file; its fix is `flisk config regenerate`.\n- **Defined in:** [`doctor-wiring.ts`](../cli/src/utils/doctor-wiring.ts) (package.json), [`doctor.ts`](../cli/src/commands/doctor.ts) (scripts, discovery, master configs, Starlark).\n- **Checked:** reproduced, each by changing one thing in a scratch project. A defect was noted in [adopt-flisk.md](adopt-flisk.md#2-pilot-in-one-afternoon): on the code that page was written against, `N resources without a package.json` was also reported for bring-your-own resources. It was not re-run for this page.\n\n### `Backends without a health route`\n\n```text\n✗ Backends without a health route (the container never turns healthy, so Traefik returns 404):\n    orders-api: no \"/health\" route in src/ (probed by the container and Traefik healthchecks)\nℹ Fix: Add the route, e.g. for Hono: app.get(\"/health\", (c) => c.json({ status: \"ok\" })). To use a different path, set \"healthCheckPath\" (container healthcheck) or \"traefik.healthCheck\" (Traefik) in its service.json.\n```\n\n- **Cause:** the check searches `src/` for the health path from `service.json` and found none. The scratch case renamed `/health` to `/healthz` in a scaffolded backend. Per the message, a service with no health route never turns healthy and Traefik answers 404 for it; that part was not observed, because `flisk up` was not run.\n- **Fix:** add the route, or point `healthCheckPath` and `traefik.healthCheck` at the path you use.\n- **Defined in:** [`doctor-runtime.ts`](../cli/src/utils/doctor-runtime.ts).\n- **Checked:** reproduced (the doctor message only).\n\n### `Unknown service.json field ...: unknown field is preserved`\n\n```text\n⚠️  <project>/services/shop/orders-api/service.json.healthCheck: unknown field is preserved\n```\n\n- **Cause:** `service.json` has a key Flisk does not know. Flisk leaves it in place. The scratch case added `\"healthCheck\": \"/health\"` by hand; doctor still exited 0.\n- **Fix:** none required. Remove the field, or fix its spelling, if you expected it to do something. [Pilot Flisk on a real repository](adopt-flisk.md#2-pilot-in-one-afternoon) records that on Flisk 1.3.86 a freshly scaffolded service produced this for `healthCheck`; a service scaffolded by the version used for this page did not.\n- **Defined in:** [`service-manifest.ts`](../cli/src/utils/service-manifest.ts). The warning's wording was `Unknown service.json field: ...` in the earlier capture and is `<path>.<field>: unknown field is preserved` now.\n- **Checked:** reproduced.\n\n### Other doctor failures, read from source\n\nAll **read from source, not reproduced**.\n\n| Message (start of it) | Cause as the message states it | Fix as printed | Defined in |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `N Tilt processes are running for this project (pids ...). They fight over ports and containers` | More than one Tilt for this project | `flisk down && kill <pids> && flisk up` | `doctor-wiring.ts` |\n| `Another project's Tilt is running: ...` | Another project's Tilt holds a UI port | `flisk up` uses the next free Tilt UI port (10351, ...); if it is a leftover, run `flisk down` in that project | `doctor-wiring.ts` |\n| `N migration command(s) run from a backend or worker's own start-up` | A service migrates on every start | Move the migration into a `migrator` resource the service depends on; see [byo](byo.md#one-shot-jobs-migrations-seeders) | `doctor-wiring.ts` |\n| `N service URL(s) in params that cannot connect` | A URL in `params` points at the wrong port | Containers reach each other as `http://<resource>:<port>`, using the `port` in that service's `service.json` | `doctor-wiring.ts` |\n| `N frontend call(s) to a backend port that Flisk does not publish on localhost` | A frontend calls `localhost:<backend port>` | Use the Traefik URL `http://api.<project>.localhost/api/<name without -api>/...` | `doctor-wiring.ts` |\n| `... enable the nats feature, but no NATS server will start` | `services/platform/messaging/docker-compose.yml` is missing and Flisk does not generate it | Create it with a `nats` service and a `redis` service, as the Fix line describes | `doctor-wiring.ts` |\n| `Generated Dockerfiles reference missing project runtime assets` | Generated files are older than the CLI | `flisk project --yes` or `flisk config regenerate` with an updated Flisk | `doctor.ts` |\n| `Starlark load() issues will stop Tilt before services start` | A generated `.star` file loads something that is missing | `flisk config regenerate` | `doctor.ts` |\n| `bun run build` will fail with TS2688 (Cannot find type definition file) | A `types` entry in the Docker tsconfig has no `@types` package in `devDependencies` | Add the packages the Fix line lists | `doctor.ts` |\n| `Frontend Docker metadata has issues that will make flisk up fail or route 404` | Generated frontend metadata is stale | `flisk config regenerate`; make sure the nginx runtime routes to port 80 | `doctor.ts` |\n| `N container(s) pinned near its memory limit while burning CPU -- likely stuck in a boot-time retry/fetch loop` | Cause is not known; the message suggests a boot-time retry loop | Read `docker logs <container>`; the Fix line names `AUTO_MIGRATE=false` as a temporary unblock when a Prisma auto-migration is the cause | `doctor.ts` |\n| `Tilt reports N failed resource(s):` | A Tilt resource failed to update | Read the resource's log in the Tilt UI or `flisk logs` | `doctor-runtime.ts` |\n| `Private npm registry (Verdaccio) is not reachable at ...` | Verdaccio is expected by the project but not answering | Start it as the Fix line says. Verdaccio needs a Premium licence ([features](FEATURES.md)) | `doctor-runtime.ts` |\n| `Tilt is not running - skipped resource status check` (a skip, not a failure) | You have not run `flisk up` | `flisk up` | `doctor-runtime.ts` |\n\n## Ports\n\n### `Traefik cannot bind ingress ports because another container already owns them`\n\n```text\n✗ Traefik cannot bind ingress ports because another container already owns them:\n    <container name> (host ports <ports>)\nℹ Fix: Stop the foreign container(s), e.g. `docker stop <container name>`, or shut down the other Flisk/Tilt project using that Traefik. Then re-run `flisk up`.\n```\n\n- **Cause:** a Docker container that is not this project's already publishes the ingress ports (80 and 443 when they are free, otherwise 8080 and 8443).\n- **Fix:** stop that container only if it is yours to stop, or move Flisk to other ports with `FLISK_HTTP_PORT`, `FLISK_HTTPS_PORT` and `FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT`. On a shared Docker daemon the container may belong to someone else.\n- **Defined in:** [`doctor-runtime.ts`](../cli/src/utils/doctor-runtime.ts) (`checkIngressPorts`).\n- **Checked:** read from source, not reproduced; it needs a second container on the ingress port.\n\n### `Ports Flisk needs are taken`\n\n```text\n✗ Ports Flisk needs are taken:\n    5432 (<name>) is used by a program on this machine; inspect it with lsof -nP -iTCP:5432\nℹ Fix: Stop local Postgres or change the host port. Then: flisk doctor\n```\n\nThe same check prints `Stop the process bound to port 80, or stop local nginx/caddy. Then: flisk doctor` when the first port found taken is 80, and for 443 `Stop the process bound to port 443. Then: flisk doctor`. The names in the middle line come from `HOST_PORTS` in the source; the exact label text was not captured.\n\n- **Cause:** something other than this project holds 5432, 80 or 443 (a local Postgres is the case the message is written for). With Docker holding 5432, the line says `is published by container <name>`.\n- **Fix:** stop the program, or choose other host ports with `FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT`. The default host ports are 80 and 443 for HTTP and HTTPS, and 15432 for Postgres (Flisk does not try 5432). Flisk picks the next free one when a default is busy; see [the FAQ](faq-teams.md#ports-and-running-two-stacks-at-once). How the 5432 check relates to the default of 15432 was not investigated.\n- **Defined in:** [`doctor-runtime.ts`](../cli/src/utils/doctor-runtime.ts) (`checkHostPorts`).\n- **Checked:** read from source, not reproduced.\n\n### `FLISK_HTTP_PORT=... is already in use`\n\n```text\n$ FLISK_HTTP_PORT=18777 flisk up shop --dry-run      # something already listening on 18777\nError: FLISK_HTTP_PORT=18777 is already in use. Choose a free host port and retry.\n\n$ FLISK_HTTP_PORT=18777 flisk doctor --no-ping\n✗ FLISK_HTTP_PORT=18777 is already in use. Choose a free host port and retry.\nℹ Fix: Set FLISK_HTTP_PORT, FLISK_HTTPS_PORT, or FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT to available host ports.\n✗ Ingress port plan is unavailable\n```\n\n- **Cause:** you set the variable explicitly and that port is taken. Flisk does not fall back to another port when you chose one, and it does not stop the program using it.\n- **Fix:** pick a free port. Without the variable, Flisk picks the next free one itself.\n- **Defined in:** [`host-port-plan.ts`](../cli/src/utils/host-port-plan.ts).\n- **Checked:** reproduced with a stand-in listener.\n\n### `No available host port in ... for ...`\n\n```text\nNo available host port in 15432-15532 for postgres. Set FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT to a free port and retry.\n```\n\n- **Cause:** every candidate port for that service is taken, usually because many projects are running. HTTP, HTTPS and Postgres each have a range of 101 ports (8080-8180, 8443-8543 and 15432-15532). Every project takes a Postgres port from its range, so at most 101 projects fit with the defaults, fewer if other programs hold ports in it. HTTP and HTTPS try 80 and 443 first and take a port from their range only when that port is not usable, so the first project does not use up a range port. The message names the first service whose candidates run out. That is `postgres` when 80 and 443 are both usable, `ingressHttp` when 80 is not (it is taken, or on Linux and WSL2 a non-root user cannot check it), and `ingressHttps` when only 443 is not.\n- **Fix:** stop projects you no longer need with `flisk down`, or set the variable the message names for this project: `FLISK_HTTP_PORT` for `ingressHttp`, `FLISK_HTTPS_PORT` for `ingressHttps`, `FLISK_POSTGRES_PORT` for `postgres`. The ranges cannot be changed. An override is used as-is: if that port is taken, Flisk reports it instead of picking another.\n- **Defined in:** [`host-port-plan.ts`](../cli/src/utils/host-port-plan.ts).\n- **Checked:** read from source, and the planner (not `flisk up`) was exercised with a stubbed port probe in `host-port-plan.test.ts`; not reproduced with real concurrent projects.\n\n### `Port 10350 is already in use` / `Auto-switching to port ...`\n\n```text\n⚠️  Port 10350 is already in use\n🔄 Auto-switching to port <port>\n```\n\n- **Cause:** another process, usually another project's Tilt, already uses Tilt's UI port 10350. Flisk uses the next free port, so the Tilt UI for this project is at that port, not 10350.\n- **Fix:** none needed; open the UI on the port printed. If the other Tilt is a leftover, `flisk down` in that project.\n- **Defined in:** [`up.ts`](../cli/src/commands/up.ts). With `flisk up --only`, a Tilt that is already running stops it with `A Tilt is already running on port ...: run `flisk down` first, or pass --force to replace it.`\n- **Checked:** read from source, not reproduced; it is printed after the dry-run exit point, so it needs a real start.\n\n### Two services on the same port, no message at all\n\n`flisk resource a --port 4000` followed by `flisk resource b --port 4000` created both resources, with `\"port\": 4000` in each `service.json`, and `flisk doctor --no-ping` passed. There is no warning. Without `--port`, `flisk resource` picks the next free port in 4000-5999. Whether two services on one port fail at `flisk up` was not checked. Use distinct ports.\n\n## Services never turn healthy, or are left behind\n\n### `HTTP 404: Traefik is answering but has no route for this URL`\n\n```text\n✗ N service(s) not healthy (M/K healthy):\n    <name> (HTTP 404: Traefik is answering but has no route for this URL (container not started yet, or not running))\n      <url>\nℹ Fix: If `flisk up` just started, images may still be building: re-run `flisk doctor` in a minute. Otherwise check `docker ps` and the resource in the Tilt UI, and compare `flisk networks` with the URL above.\n```\n\nA 502, 503 or 504 prints `Traefik found the route but the container isn't answering (still starting, or unhealthy)` instead.\n\n- **Cause:** per the message, Traefik is up and no running container is registered for that URL. The reasons the source gives are a service still building or not started. [Backends without a health route](#backends-without-a-health-route) is one way a container never becomes healthy.\n- **Fix:** wait and re-run `flisk doctor`; otherwise check the resource in the Tilt UI and `flisk networks`.\n- **Seen once with a puzzle:** during earlier work on this repository, doctor printed this 404 right after startup for a URL that had already answered 200 when fetched directly. That was not investigated, so a 404 right after startup is not proof the service is broken.\n- **Defined in:** [`doctor-runtime.ts`](../cli/src/utils/doctor-runtime.ts) (`describeProbeFailure`, `summarizeServiceProbes`).\n- **Checked:** read from source, not reproduced. A 404 stub on the ingress port could not be tried, because Flisk moved to the next free port while the stub was listening.\n\n### `flisk down` left containers running\n\nBefore [#585](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/pull/585), `flisk up shop` started Tilt with `--focus=shop` but `flisk down` ran `tilt down` without it, so the stack's own containers were never removed, and a live `tilt up` could re-create a container right after it was removed. The PR changed `flisk down` to pass every discovered stack, to stop this project's own `tilt up` first, and added `flisk down --prune-networks`. Networks created by `init-networks` are kept after `flisk down` unless you pass it.\n\n- **Fix:** use a version that includes #585, then `flisk down`. On an older version, `docker ps` shows what is left; stop only containers whose names start with your project's.\n- **Checked:** read from the PR description and merged change, not reproduced.\n\n## Scaffolded code\n\n### Frontend `bun run build` stops with `.autogenerated/vite.config... does not exist yet`\n\n```text\n$ bun run build          # in a scaffolded frontend, before any flisk up\n.autogenerated/vite.config.build.autogenerated.ts does not exist yet. Flisk engine writes it when flisk up loads this resource (needs Docker); flisk resource and flisk config regenerate do not create it. Run flisk up <stack> once, or point this script at your own Vite config to build on the host.\nerror: script \"prebuild\" exited with code 1\n```\n\n- **Cause:** by design. The Vite config is written by the engine when `flisk up` loads the resource, not by `flisk resource`. `dev` has the same guard with `vite.config.frontend.autogenerated.ts`.\n- **Fix:** run `flisk up <stack>` once, or point the script at your own Vite config.\n- **Defined in:** [`resource.ts`](../cli/src/commands/resource.ts) (`requireGeneratedViteConfig`).\n- **Checked:** reproduced (`prebuild` only; the exit code of the script is 1).\n\n### `TS2591: Cannot find name 'process'` in an older scaffold\n\n```text\nsrc/index.ts(33,14): error TS2591: Cannot find name 'process'.\n```\n\n- **Cause:** older scaffolds had no `types` in `tsconfig.json`, and TypeScript 7 does not include `@types/*` on its own. The Docker image was not affected, because it uses the engine's own tsconfig. Fixed for new scaffolds in [#586](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/pull/586). That PR also records `Cannot find name 'Buffer'` for an `mcp` scaffold.\n- **Fix:** in an older service, add `\"types\": [\"node\"]` under `compilerOptions` in `tsconfig.json` by hand. A service scaffolded by the current code already has it.\n- **Checked:** the current scaffold was seen to contain `\"types\": [\"node\"]`; the error itself was **read from the PR, not reproduced**, because it needs an older scaffold.\n\n## Windows and WSL2\n\nNative Windows can run `flisk doctor` and inspect a project; starting needs WSL2. Setup and the smoke run are in [WSL2 setup](wsl2.md#troubleshooting). All entries here are **read from source, not reproduced** (no Windows machine was used).\n\n| Message | Fix as printed | Defined in |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `Native Windows landscape boot is unsupported. Use WSL2 Ubuntu with Docker Desktop integration. Guide: docs/wsl2.md` | Use WSL2 Ubuntu with Docker Desktop integration | `doctor.ts` |\n| `Docker Desktop is using Windows containers. Switch to Linux containers. Flisk stacks are Linux images.` | Switch Docker Desktop to Linux containers | `windows-doctor.ts` |\n| `tilt.exe not found on PATH. Install Tilt from https://docs.tilt.dev/install.html` | Install Tilt | `windows-doctor.ts` |\n| `Windows reserved port(s) ... for Hyper-V/WSL ...` | The message suggests `net stop winnat` then `net start winnat` in an Administrator terminal, or changing Flisk's ports | `windows-doctor.ts` |\n| `Windows did not resolve *.flisk.localhost to 127.0.0.1. Browser URLs with hostnames will fail until you fix hosts or use the 127.0.0.1 URLs printed by flisk networks.` | Fix hosts, or use the `127.0.0.1` URLs from `flisk networks` | `windows-doctor.ts` |\n| `WSL2 detected. Use Docker Desktop WSL integration. Guide: docs/wsl2.md ... Project is under /mnt/c (...); hot reload may be broken.` (a warning; a failure with `--strict`) | Move the repository under your WSL home directory, for example `~/projects` | `doctor.ts` |\n| Docker unreachable from WSL2 (`docker version` cannot reach the server) | Enable the distribution under Docker Desktop **Resources → WSL Integration** and restart Ubuntu ([wsl2.md](wsl2.md#troubleshooting)) | `wsl2.md` |\n\nThe same source files hold messages for [agent hosts](agent-hosts.md) such as Dev Containers and WebContainers (`agent-host.ts`); they were not collected here.\n\n## How to ask for help\n\nIf the page does not cover it, or the fix did not work:\n\n1. Run `flisk --version` and `flisk doctor` in the project, and keep the output. Add `--no-ping` if no stack is running.\n2. Open a [bug report](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/issues/new?template=bug_report.yml). It asks for the `flisk doctor` output, the Flisk version and your operating system, and for the steps to reproduce.\n3. Include the exact message, not a paraphrase, and say whether `flisk up` got as far as starting Tilt.\n4. For questions that are not bugs, and for what to expect from maintainers, see [SUPPORT.md](../.github/SUPPORT.md).\n\nDo not paste your `.env`: it holds a generated database password and JWT secret.\n",
  "wsl2.md": "# Flisk on Windows\n\nOn Windows, you run Flisk inside the **Ubuntu** app. Ubuntu is a free version of Linux, and [WSL2](start/words.md#wsl2) (Windows Subsystem for Linux) is the part of Windows that runs it, next to your usual Windows apps. You install it once with one command, then type every Flisk command in the Ubuntu app, which is your [terminal](start/words.md#terminal) on Windows.\n\nWhy the extra step? Flisk starts your app with Linux tools: Linux [containers](start/words.md#container), [Docker](start/words.md#docker) and [Tilt](start/words.md#tilt). Windows cannot run Linux containers without WSL2.\n\nJust want the short version? [Set up Windows](setup/windows.md) has three steps. Never written code? [Start here](start/first-time.md). This page has every step in detail, an example app to try, and fixes for common problems.\n\n## Support summary\n\nWhich Flisk commands work where. In short: on Windows, use the Ubuntu app for everything. Windows on its own (\"native Windows\") can check your computer and show what Flisk would start, but cannot start your app.\n\n| Platform | `flisk up` | `flisk doctor` | `flisk --version` | `flisk up --dry-run` | Notes |\n|----------|------------|----------------|-------------------|----------------------|-------|\n| macOS / Linux | ✅ Full runtime | ✅ Full | ✅ | ✅ | Native support |\n| **WSL2 Ubuntu** | ✅ Full runtime | ✅ Full | ✅ | ✅ | **Recommended for Windows** |\n| Native Windows | ❌ Refused | ✅ Inspection only | ✅ | ✅ Inspection only | See below |\n\n**Bottom line**: Run Flisk from **Ubuntu inside WSL2** for the full stack. Native Windows runs only inspection commands (`flisk --version`, `flisk doctor`, `flisk up --dry-run`); `flisk up` exits with a clear refusal pointing to WSL2.\n\n## Recommended setup: WSL2 Ubuntu with Docker Desktop\n\n### Prerequisites\n- Windows 10 22H2+ or Windows 11 (WSL2 requires Windows 10 1903+; 22H2+ recommended for best compatibility)\n- WSL2 installed: `wsl --install -d Ubuntu` (or `wsl --install` for default Ubuntu)\n- Docker Desktop for Windows with **WSL2 backend** selected\n- Admin rights for Docker Desktop installation\n\n### Step-by-step\n\nDo these in order. Each grey box is a [command](start/words.md#command): paste it into the Ubuntu app and press Enter.\n\n1. **Install Docker Desktop with WSL2 backend**\n   - Download from [docker.com](https://www.docker.com/products/docker-desktop/)\n   - During install, ensure **Use WSL 2 instead of Hyper-V** is checked\n   - After install, open Docker Desktop → **Settings → Resources → WSL Integration** → enable for your Ubuntu distro\n   - Click **Apply & Restart**\n\n2. **Open Ubuntu (WSL2)**\n   - Launch \"Ubuntu\" from Windows Terminal or Start menu\n   - Verify Docker is reachable:\n     ```sh\n     docker version\n     docker compose version\n     ```\n     Both should print version info without errors.\n\n3. **Install Tilt and Flisk in Ubuntu**\n   ```sh\n   curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tilt-dev/tilt/master/scripts/install.sh | bash\n   curl -fsSL https://flisk.sh/install.sh | sh\n   ```\n   Or use Bun/npm:\n   ```sh\n   bun add -g @flisk-sh/flisk\n   # or\n   npm install -g @flisk-sh/flisk\n   ```\n\n4. **Verify the environment**\n   ```sh\n   flisk doctor\n   ```\n   All checks should pass. If any fail, see [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting) below.\n\n5. **Boot the example stack**\n   ```sh\n   flisk project example --path \"$HOME/flisk-example\"\n   cd \"$HOME/flisk-example\"\n   flisk project --yes\n   sed -i 's/^INFISICAL_ENABLED=.*/INFISICAL_ENABLED=false/' .env\n   flisk config verify\n   flisk doctor\n   flisk up shop\n   ```\n   The command prints the HTTP port (80, or 8080-8180 on WSL2). Use the printed URLs to access services.\n\n6. **Verify the stack works** (in another Ubuntu terminal)\n   ```sh\n   export HTTP_PORT=<port printed by flisk up>\n   curl -fsS \"http://api.flisk-example.localhost:$HTTP_PORT/api/orders/health\"\n   curl -fsS \"http://app.flisk-example.localhost:$HTTP_PORT\"\n   ```\n\n7. **Clean up**\n   Press `Ctrl+C` in the `flisk up` terminal, then run:\n   ```sh\n   flisk down\n   ```\n\n## The one-command demo (WSL2 only)\n\nThe shortest path to a running stack:\n\n```sh\nflisk demo\n```\n\nThis clones the starter, installs dependencies, boots the stack, and opens the frontend. **Only works in WSL2 Ubuntu**; native Windows refuses with WSL2 guidance.\n\n## Native Windows: what works and what doesn't\n\n| Command | Native Windows | WSL2 Ubuntu |\n|---------|----------------|-------------|\n| `flisk --version` | ✅ Prints version | ✅ |\n| `flisk doctor` | ✅ Inspection checks | ✅ Full checks |\n| `flisk up --dry-run` | ✅ Shows what would start | ✅ |\n| `flisk up <stack>` | ❌ Exits 1 with WSL2 guidance | ✅ Boots stack |\n| `flisk demo` | ❌ Refuses before cloning | ✅ Full demo flow |\n\n**Why?** `flisk up` needs Linux containers, Docker, and Tilt. Native Windows cannot run Linux containers without WSL2. The CLI refuses early to avoid partial/confusing state.\n\nIf you are on native Windows and see:\n```\nLandscape startup needs Ubuntu on WSL2. Native Windows is inspect-only.\n```\n→ Open **Ubuntu (WSL2)** and run the commands there.\n\n## Verification checklist\n\nAfter setup, run these to confirm readiness:\n\n```sh\n# 1. Docker reachable\ndocker ps\n\n# 2. Tilt installed\ntilt version\n\n# 3. Flisk installed\nflisk --version\n\n# 4. Doctor passes\nflisk doctor\n\n# 5. Config verifies (in a project)\nflisk config verify\n```\n\nAll should succeed without errors. If not, see [Troubleshooting](#troubleshooting).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n| Symptom | Cause | Fix |\n|---------|-------|-----|\n| `docker version` fails / \"Cannot connect to the Docker daemon\" | WSL integration not enabled | Docker Desktop → Settings → Resources → WSL Integration → enable Ubuntu → Apply & Restart → restart Ubuntu |\n| `flisk doctor` shows port 80/443/5432 conflict | Another process holds the port | `netstat -ano \\| findstr :80` (Windows) or `ss -tlnp \\| grep :80` (Ubuntu) → stop the process or set `FLISK_HTTP_PORT` |\n| `flisk up` picks port 8080+ instead of 80 | Non-root user cannot bind port 80 on Linux/WSL2 | Expected behavior; use the printed URL with the port. Or run `sudo setcap 'cap_net_bind_service=+ep' $(which docker)` (not recommended) |\n| `flisk doctor` warns about `FLISK_BIND_ADDRESS` | Wildcard bind address exposes ports to network | Unset `FLISK_BIND_ADDRESS` to keep ports on `127.0.0.1` |\n| `flisk up` fails with \"Tilt not found\" | Tilt not in PATH | `curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/tilt-dev/tilt/master/scripts/install.sh \\| bash` then restart shell |\n| `bun: command not found` | Bun not installed | `curl -fsSL https://bun.sh/install \\| bash` or install via npm |\n\n## What is NOT supported\n\n| Feature | Status | Notes |\n|---------|--------|-------|\n| Native Windows `flisk up` | Not supported | No Linux container runtime; use WSL2 |\n| Docker Desktop without WSL2 backend | Not supported | Hyper-V backend has different networking; use WSL2 backend |\n| Windows Containers | Not supported | Flisk generates Linux container configs only |\n| ARM64 Windows | Untested | AMD64 only for now |\n\n## CI evidence\n\nFlisk runs automated WSL2 smoke tests on GitHub-hosted `windows-2022` runners with Ubuntu 24.04 WSL2 and Docker Engine inside the distro. The workflow runs on every push to `main` and is a required check.\n\n- Workflow: [🐧 Platforms WSL2 E2E](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/actions/workflows/e2e-platforms-wsl2.yml)\n- **Note**: CI uses **Docker Engine inside WSL2**, not Docker Desktop WSL integration. The guide above describes Docker Desktop integration; the two paths are functionally similar but not identical.\n\n## See also\n\n- [Windows hardware checks](windows-hardware.md): Evidence from real Windows machines\n- [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.md): General troubleshooting guide\n- [Requirements and support](../README.md#requirements-and-support): Platform support matrix\n- [Issue #107](https://github.com/flisk-sh/flisk/issues/107): Windows/WSL2 quickstart test tracking\n"
};
